/*! \page page_b3xx_kernel B3xx Kernel Module Installation and Usage

\tableofcontents

\section b3xx_kernel_linux Installing the Linux Kernel Module

\subsection b3xx_kernel_linux_overview Overview

The B310 PCIe transport on Linux requires the `nib310rio` kernel module.
Without this module, UHD cannot discover or use the B310 device over the PCIe or Thunderbolt interface.

\subsection b3xx_kernel_linux_requirements Requirements

- Linux kernel headers for the running kernel
- Build tools (`gcc`, `make`)
- `dkms` (recommended for package-based installs)

Example (Ubuntu/Debian):

    sudo apt update
    sudo apt install -y dkms gcc make linux-headers-$(uname -r)

\subsection b3xx_kernel_linux_install_desktop Linux Desktop Installation

For standard Linux desktop systems (for example Ubuntu or Fedora), install the
`nib310rio` driver packages and ensure the module is loaded.

Download the latest Linux kernel driver packages from
<a href="https://files.ettus.com/binaries/uhd/latest_release">Ettus UHD latest release binaries</a>.


The package names and roles are:

- `nib310rio-dkms`: Required runtime kernel driver package. This package builds
	and installs the `nib310rio` kernel module via DKMS.
- `nib310rio-dev`: Development header package. This installs
	`/usr/include/nib310rio/nib310rio.h`, which is required to compile B310
	support in UHD.

Example install flow (Debian/Ubuntu):

    sudo dpkg -i nib310rio-dkms_<version>_all.deb
    sudo dpkg -i nib310rio-dev_<version>_all.deb

For Fedora/RHEL-like systems, install the equivalent RPM packages:

    sudo dnf install ./nib310rio-dkms-<version>-1.noarch.rpm
    sudo dnf install ./nib310rio-dev-<version>-1.noarch.rpm

After installation, validate that the module is present:

    lsmod | grep nib310rio

If needed, load it manually:

    sudo modprobe nib310rio

Then verify that UHD can discover the device:

    uhd_find_devices --args type=b3xx

\subsection b3xx_kernel_linux_troubleshooting Linux Package Troubleshooting

The checks below summarize common package-installation failures and validation
steps observed in the B310 Linux driver package installation flow.

\subsubsection b3xx_kernel_linux_troubleshooting_deps Dependency issues

If `dkms` install reports missing `pahole`:

    sudo apt install -y pahole
    sudo apt install -y dkms

If `dpkg -i` leaves `nib310rio-dkms` unconfigured due to unmet dependencies:

    sudo apt --fix-broken install -y
    sudo dpkg --configure -a

\subsubsection b3xx_kernel_linux_troubleshooting_validate Validate DKMS and module placement

After package installation, validate DKMS state and module location:

    dkms status | grep nib310rio
    modinfo -n nib310rio

Expected:

- `dkms status` reports `nib310rio` installed for the running kernel.
- `modinfo -n nib310rio` resolves to a path under `/lib/modules/...`.

\subsubsection b3xx_kernel_linux_troubleshooting_output Sample package installation output

The following abbreviated output snippets are examples of successful package
installation.

Debian/Ubuntu (`nib310rio-dkms`):

~~~
$ sudo dpkg -i nib310rio-dkms_<version>_all.deb
Setting up nib310rio-dkms (<version>) ...
Building module:
...
nib310rio.ko:
...
Installing to /lib/modules/<kernel>/updates/dkms/
depmod...
~~~

Fedora/RHEL (`nib310rio-dkms`):

~~~
$ sudo dnf install ./nib310rio-dkms-<version>-1.noarch.rpm
Installing:
  nib310rio-dkms-<version>-1.noarch
...
Building module(s)... done.
Installing /lib/modules/<kernel>/.../nib310rio.ko.xz
Running depmod... done.
Complete!
~~~

\subsubsection b3xx_kernel_linux_troubleshooting_secureboot Secure Boot considerations

On Secure Boot systems, module build/install may succeed but `modprobe` can fail
with key rejection until a trusted key is enrolled and the module is rebuilt and
re-signed. If this occurs, follow your distribution's MOK enrollment flow, then
rebuild/reinstall the DKMS module.

Typical error:

~~~
modprobe: ERROR: could not insert 'nib310rio': Key was rejected by service
~~~

On Debian/Ubuntu systems, the flow is typically:

1. Install MOK tooling:

	sudo apt install -y shim-signed mokutil

2. Reboot and enroll the key in MOK Manager:
   - Select `Enroll MOK`.
   - Select `Continue`.
   - Select `Yes`.
   - Enter the enrollment password set during `shim-signed` installation.
   - Select `Reboot`.

3. Rebuild and reinstall the DKMS module after enrollment:

	sudo dkms remove nib310rio/<version> --all || true
	sudo dkms add -m nib310rio -v <version>
	sudo dkms build -m nib310rio -v <version> -k "$(uname -r)"
	sudo dkms install -m nib310rio -v <version> -k "$(uname -r)" --force
	sudo depmod -a
	sudo modprobe nib310rio

4. Verify Secure Boot state, module signer, and module load:

	mokutil --sb-state
	modinfo nib310rio | grep -i signer
	lsmod | grep nib310rio

\subsubsection b3xx_kernel_linux_troubleshooting_artifacts Validate installed artifacts

Confirm that expected packaging artifacts are present:

    test -f /etc/udev/rules.d/99-nib310rio.rules && echo "udev rule present"
    test -f /etc/modules-load.d/nib310rio.conf && echo "autoload config present"
    test -f /usr/include/nib310rio/nib310rio.h && echo "dev header present"

\subsection b3xx_kernel_linux_jetson Jetson AGX Orin

This section targets L4T 36.4.4 / JetPack 6.x.

On Jetson AGX Orin, the B310 software path requires Linux DMA-BUF heap support.
In particular, `/dev/dma_heap/system` must be available. If this device node is
missing, DMA-based operation will fail and only limited BAR0 access may work.

\subsubsection b3xx_kernel_linux_jetson_outline Recommended Jetson workflow

Use the following sequence when enabling B310 support on Jetson:

1. Enable DMA-BUF feature support (if missing) and verify `/dev/dma_heap/system`.
2. Install the `nib310rio` kernel driver packages as in the Linux desktop case
	(see \ref b3xx_kernel_linux_install_desktop).
3. Build and test UHD on Jetson.

\subsubsection b3xx_kernel_linux_jetson_version Verify Jetson software version

Confirm the target release on the Jetson host:

    uname -r
    cat /etc/nv_tegra_release

\subsubsection b3xx_kernel_linux_jetson_dmabuf Enable DMA-BUF heap support

NVIDIA kernel customization reference:
<a href="https://docs.nvidia.com/jetson/archives/r36.4.4/DeveloperGuide/SD/Kernel/KernelCustomization.html">Jetson Linux Kernel Customization</a>

The procedure below builds the kernel natively on the Jetson and installs it on
that same device. A cross-compiled kernel must instead be installed through the
Jetson Linux `Linux_for_Tegra` flashing workflow; do not run the `/boot` copy or
`modules_install` commands below on the cross-build host.

Build environment setup and source checkout:

    sudo apt update
    sudo apt install -y git build-essential bc flex bison libssl-dev zstd

Download and extract the Jetson Linux release package and public sources for the
target L4T release from the
<a href="https://developer.nvidia.com/embedded/jetson-linux-archive">Jetson Linux archive</a>.
The release must match the version reported by `cat /etc/nv_tegra_release`.
In the commands below, `<install-path>` is a directory on the Jetson, for
example `~/jetson_linux`.
For L4T 36.4.4, use the Jetson Linux 36.4.4 package and its corresponding
release tag. Then sync the kernel sources:

    cd <install-path>/Linux_for_Tegra/source
    ./source_sync.sh -k -t <release-tag>
    export ARCH=arm64
    export LOCALVERSION=-tegra
    make -C kernel tegra_defconfig

If Git source synchronization is unavailable, download `public_sources.tbz2`
from the same archive and extract the matching source archives instead:

    cd <install-path>/Linux_for_Tegra/source
    tar xf kernel_src.tbz2
    tar xf kernel_oot_modules_src.tbz2
    tar xf nvidia_kernel_display_driver_source.tbz2
    export ARCH=arm64
    export LOCALVERSION=-tegra
    make -C kernel tegra_defconfig

Open kernel configuration:

    make -C kernel menuconfig

Enable these options as built-ins (`=y`):

    CONFIG_DMA_SHARED_BUFFER=y
    CONFIG_DMABUF_HEAPS=y
    CONFIG_DMABUF_HEAPS_SYSTEM=y
    CONFIG_SYNC_FILE=y

Build and install:

    make -C kernel -j$(nproc)
    sudo make -C kernel modules_install
    sudo cp /boot/Image /boot/Image.backup
    sudo cp kernel/kernel-jammy-src/arch/arm64/boot/Image /boot/Image
    sudo depmod -a
    sudo update-initramfs -u
    sudo reboot

\subsubsection b3xx_kernel_linux_jetson_verify Verify DMA-BUF after reboot

Check kernel config:

    zgrep DMABUF /proc/config.gz

Verify DMA heap node exists:

    ls -l /dev/dma_heap/

Expected: `system` entry under `/dev/dma_heap/`.

Check kernel log:

    dmesg | grep dma_heap

Expected log includes registration of the system heap.

Once DMA-BUF support is enabled and verified, use the same `nib310rio`, UHD, and
FPGA image flow as the Linux desktop case.

\subsubsection b3xx_kernel_linux_jetson_uhd Build and test UHD on Jetson

Build and install UHD using the same process as Linux desktop builds
(see \ref page_build_guide).

After UHD and the driver are installed, validate operation:

    uhd_find_devices --args type=b3xx
    uhd_usrp_probe --args type=b3xx

\section b3xx_kernel_windows Installing the Windows Kernel Driver

On Windows, install the `b310k` MSI driver package `b310ki_win64.msi` from
<a href="https://files.ettus.com/binaries/uhd/latest_release">Ettus UHD latest release binaries</a>.

After installation, confirm the B310 appears in Device Manager and is enabled.
If required, disable/enable the device from Device Manager to recover from
enumeration failures after cabling or power changes.

*/
